# Git ships a built-in "rust" diff driver whose xfuncname regex knows what a
# Rust item declaration looks like. Without this line `git check-attr diff`
# reports "unspecified" for *.rs, git falls back to a generic funcname
# heuristic that does not recognise `fn`, and function-scoped line-log fails
# outright rather than returning nothing:
#
#   $ git log -L :from_str:days/2015-12-05/src/lib.rs
#   fatal: -L parameter 'from_str' starting at line 1: no match
#
# With it, `git log -L :<fn>:<file>` walks the history of just that function,
# and hunk headers name the enclosing item instead of showing `@@ ... @@` bare.
*.rs diff=rust

# days/2015-12-01/wasm/test/banner.test.ts compares figlet.js's rows against
# libcaca's, recorded in this fixture, byte for byte. Git for Windows checks
# text files out with CRLF by default (core.autocrlf=true, which the wasm32
# CI job's windows-latest runner inherits), and a fixture split on "\n" then
# carries a trailing "\r" on every row: the first wasm32 run on Windows died
# in that test with `actual: '🦀:\r', expected: '🦀:'`. eol=lf keeps the
# recorded bytes on every platform. The .flf font next to it survives CRLF
# (figlet.js normalises while parsing), so it gets no rule.
days/2015-12-01/wasm/test/fixtures/* text eol=lf
